<title>SOUTH KOREA: South Korean strike status at 0930 GMT.</title>
<headline>South Korean strike status at 0930 GMT.</headline>
<dateline>SEOUL 1997-01-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>South Korea has been hit by a series of strikes since December 26 against a controversial labour law that allows employers to lay off workers, set flexible work hours and use part-time labour.</p>
<p>Following are the latest figures available on South Korea's strike. The numbers are provided by the Korean Confederation of Trade Unions spearheading industrial action since December 26 when the ruling party rammed through parliament a new labour law that allows troubled companies to lay off workers.</p>
<p>(Figures in brackets are Ministry of Labour estimates of actual workers on strike against the total workforce)</p>
<p> SECTOR	 STRIKING UNIONS		   STRIKERS</p>
<p>-----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>Construction    31				    17,774</p>
<p>-----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>Metal	     24				    36,475</p>
<p>Halla Heavy - 3,326 strikers/ partial (250/5,800)</p>
<p>-----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>University	19				     3,376</p>
<p>-----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>Hospital	  39				    20,738</p>
<p>Seoul National - 2,093 strikers (300/4,000)</p>
<p>-----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>Clerical	   7				     3,500</p>
<p>-----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>Media		9				     8,337</p>
<p>Korea Broadcasting - 4,800 strikers (2,500/6,224)</p>
<p>Munwha Broadcasting - 2,200 strikers (1,800/3,289)</p>
<p>-----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>Insurance	  1				     4,700</p>
<p>-----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>Automobile	15 (excludes Hyundai Motor)   40,912</p>
<p>Kia Motors - 17,000 strikers (18,498/29,389)</p>
<p>Asia Motors - 5,627 strikers/ partial (5,618/9,159)</p>
<p>Ssangyong Motor - 7,835 strikers/ partial (6,492/11,454)</p>
<p>-----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>Professional    30				    11,578</p>
<p>-----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>Other		5					 832</p>
<p>-----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>Hyundai Union    8				    75,150</p>
<p>Hyundai Motor - 34,008 strikers/ partial (27,000/41,125)</p>
<p>*Hyundai Heavy - 21,700 strikers/ partial (0/27,000)</p>
<p>Hyundai Motor Svc - 11,000 strikers/ partial (10,000/14,488) *</p>
<p>Note: Hyundai Heavy management says work back to normal</p>
<p>-----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>Chemical	  10				     1,164</p>
<p>-----------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>TOTAL	    196				   200,636</p>
<p>The Ministry of Labour puts total number of strikers at 93,566 from 70 companies.</p>
<p>NOTE - The confederation says strikers at two Hyundai firms, Hyundai Heavy Industries and Hyundai Mipo Dock Yard, are counted twice for both metal and Hyundai union sectors.</p>
